implementationPlan.description:
TESTS FIRST (red). Load test-driven-development.

Prerequisites, landed by WORKING time; adapt to their landed names:
- CQ-I03a's producer, scripts/local-runner/vm-control.mjs, with schemas moe-vm-status-v1, moe-vm-reconcile-v1 and moe-vm-cleanup-v1;
- CQ-I03's JetBrains presentation rules. Read its handoff memory 'task-task-63930ba4a46344f9af3c41cfef60ae0d-handoff' and mirror the same behavior.

Create moe-vscode/tests/managed-vm.test.cjs (node:test), compiling src/util/ManagedVm.ts with typescript's transpileModule the way tests/settings-wire.test.cjs does. Keep it to about 130 lines. Assert exact strings, arrays and booleans; never truthiness.

Cases, mirroring I03's P1-P5:
- V1, explicit owned lifecycle status (DoD 1):
  - three distinct readiness lines: provider, execution and verified. Only a literal JSON true makes execution or verified ready, and provider ready never implies either;
  - rows equal the report's instances (instance, phase, state, launchTries, taskId), in order;
  - a name failing R06's owned-name shape, or a duplicated name, makes the whole report Unreadable.
- V2, no strict enablement (DoD 2):
  - strongProfile(report) is the fixed 'Strong profile (verified-local) is unavailable …' line for EVERY report, including one claiming verified:true;
  - actions(report, running) has exactly { refresh, reconcile, cleanup }. reconcile and cleanup are false unless provider is ready (cleanup also needs at least 1 instance), and everything is false while running.
- V3, the process seam: runVmControl(execFileImpl, node, script, command, root, timeoutMs) calls execFileImpl with exactly (node, [script, 'status'|'reconcile'|'cleanup', '--project', root], { timeout, maxBuffer: 65537, windowsHide: true }). There is no instance argument, no shell option, and stderr is ignored. These give Unreadable and never reject:
  - an execFile error;
  - a timeout;
  - exceeding maxBuffer;
  - a nonzero exit;
  - non-JSON;
  - more than one line;
  - an unknown schema.
- V4, report mapping: a cleanup report renders deleted, preserved (with reasons) and errors (with codes); an unknown code renders as 'Unknown code <code>'. The raw stdout text never appears.

In tests/settings-wire.test.cjs (DoD 3), add ONE case using its existing harness:
- the client state holds settings with executionProfile 'observe', deliveryPolicy 'local-branch', checkEnvironments [ENV] and repairPolicy {maxCodeRepairs: 2};
- saving sends UPDATE_SETTINGS with exactly the six form keys, so no profile or evidence key is sent or overwritten;
- after a SETTINGS_UPDATED full-project payload, the client state still carries all four fields unchanged (deepStrictEqual).

Run `cd moe-vscode && npm test`: the new cases fail before step 2. The settings case may already pass: if so, keep it as the DoD 3 pin and say so in the step note.

Create moe-vscode/src/util/ManagedVm.ts. It is pure: it never imports vscode, and it is about 100 lines.

Exports:
- types ManagedVmReport (Status, Reconcile, Cleanup, or Unreadable(reason)) and ManagedVmRow;
- decode(stdout, exitCode, timedOut);
- readiness(report) gives three strings, worded like I03's MoeBundle text, for example 'Provider ready', 'Execution not ready: isolation and evidence gates are not certified', 'Not verified';
- strongProfile(report);
- rows(report);
- actions(report, running);
- commandArgs(script, command, root);
- runVmControl(execFileImpl, node, script, command, root, timeoutMs): Promise<ManagedVmReport>, which wraps execFileImpl in a Promise that always resolves.

Error handling: decode and runVmControl never throw. Every malformed, foreign-named, duplicated, oversized, multi-line, unknown-schema, nonzero-exit, timed-out or spawn-failed result is Unreadable with a fixed reason. Raw stdout and stderr never reach a rendered string.

The header comment states:
- provider readiness is not execution readiness or verification;
- the extension never offers strong-profile activation, because the daemon refuses verified-local with EXECUTION_PROFILE_NOT_READY;
- ownership is the producer's ledger, and the extension never names an instance.

Make V1-V4 pass.

Wire the native TreeView. A webview is not used: no HTML, no media script, and so no HTML-injection surface. Report every file in modifiedFiles.

1. Create moe-vscode/src/providers/ManagedVmTreeProvider.ts (about 75 lines), a vscode.TreeDataProvider.
   - Root nodes:
     - the three readiness lines;
     - the strong-profile line;
     - 'Profile: <executionProfile or legacy> · Delivery: <deliveryPolicy or legacy>', read-only, from the daemon client's current project settings;
     - 'Owned VMs (n)', with one child per row;
     - the last action's report lines.
   - refresh(), reconcile() and cleanup() run ManagedVm.runVmControl:
     - execFile from child_process;
     - the node path from MoeDaemonClient's existing runtime resolver (make resolveDaemonRuntime public, with no behavior change);
     - the script at path.join(extensionPath, 'bundled', 'scripts', 'local-runner', 'vm-control.mjs'), which bundle-assets.mjs already copies;
     - the root is the workspace project path the client uses.
   - Single flight. cleanup asks a modal confirmation that names the rule (only Moe-owned VMs whose attempt is closed and whose lease is free), then runs under vscode.window.withProgress, cancellable, with a 20-minute timeout. Status and reconcile use 120s.
   - A missing script or node renders 'Managed VM control unavailable: <reason>' and starts no process.
2. moe-vscode/src/extension.ts: registerTreeDataProvider('moe.managedVms', provider) plus the commands moe.managedVms.refresh, moe.managedVms.reconcile and moe.managedVms.cleanup, pushed to context.subscriptions. Refresh once on activation.
3. moe-vscode/package.json contributes:
   - the view moe.managedVms, titled 'Managed VMs', in the existing Moe view container;
   - the three commands;
   - view/title menu entries for them.
   Add NO command or setting that edits executionProfile or deliveryPolicy.
4. moe-vscode/src/services/MoeDaemonClient.ts: only the visibility change of resolveDaemonRuntime.

Error handling: no IO on the extension host's render path, apart from the async runs. Every failure renders a fixed string. Disposal cancels a running action.

VERIFY ONCE. Edit only what this step's findings need, and report it in modifiedFiles.

1. Mutants. Apply each to a scratch copy, restore it, then cmp. Each must fail the named case:
   - derive execution readiness from provider readiness, or accept "true": V1;
   - accept a foreign or duplicate name: V1;
   - let strongProfile follow report.verified: V2;
   - enable cleanup while running: V2;
   - add an instance argument, or shell:true: V3;
   - put stderr or raw stdout into a line: V4;
   - spread stored settings into the UPDATE_SETTINGS payload: the settings-wire DoD 3 case.
2. The GATE: `cd moe-vscode && npm test && npm run compile` must exit 0, run fresh. Confirm that the managed-vm tests ran, with their counts, and that 0 failed.
3. Size: `git diff --numstat` over moe-vscode must be at most 400 net (QA cap; tests count). The budget is: test about 130, settings case about 20, ManagedVm about 100, tree about 75, package.json about 25 and extension about 8.
4. A red test outside this task is compared against a run WITHOUT this task's files. Never edit peer files.
5. Supply the command, its exit code and the output tail to complete_task.

ADVERSARIAL SELF-REVIEW, once, as the last step. Load adversarial-self-review. Read the diff as a user, or a buggy producer, trying to get VS Code to act on a VM Moe does not own, or to enable strict execution:
- a foreign or duplicated name;
- a report that claims readiness or verification;
- a double invoke of a command, or disposal mid-run;
- a huge, hanging or stderr-flooding producer;
- paths with spaces and Windows drive letters. execFile takes an args array and never a shell;
- any code path that sends UPDATE_SETTINGS with executionProfile or deliveryPolicy. There must be none;
- settings fields discarded on SETTINGS_UPDATED;
- parity with JetBrains I03: the same three lines, the no-activation rule, the owned-only rows and the confirmation wording;
- rails: no .moe writes, no Git writes, none of the project's forbidden-pattern tokens (globalRails.forbiddenPatterns), and verified-local untouched.

Then write the Serena memory 'task-task-758bb59a61bd443382e02aea44b515c9-handoff' for CQ-I06. Cover:
- the VS Code tree contract;
- the shared producer contract;
- the DoD 3 roundtrip pin;
- residuals: no real VM or IDE-host certification, and CI wiring of the producer test is still owed by I06.

planningNotes.codebaseInsights:
Verified:
- moe-vscode tests are node:test .cjs files that transpile TS with typescript.transpileModule (tests/settings-wire.test.cjs);
- `npm test` = node --test tests/*.test.cjs, and compile = tsc -p ./;
- MoeDaemonClient:
  - updateSettings sends UPDATE_SETTINGS with a Partial at line 537;
  - SETTINGS_UPDATED sets state.project = payload at line 988;
  - resolveDaemonRuntime() is private at line 733 and returns { node, env };
  - resolveBundledDaemonPath uses extensionPath/bundled;
- scripts/bundle-assets.mjs copies scripts/local-runner into bundled/scripts/local-runner, excluding tests;
- extension.ts registers webview views moe.board, moe.chat and moe.metrics, plus the commands;
- no open task edits moe-vscode files.

Assumed from CQ-I03a and CQ-I03 (approved plans, unlanded):
- the CLI `<status|reconcile|cleanup> --project <abs>` prints one JSON line with the schemas moe-vm-status-v1, moe-vm-reconcile-v1 and moe-vm-cleanup-v1;
- owned names are 'moe-vm-' plus a random suffix;
- I03's three readiness lines and its always-blocked strong profile.
